            <ArticleTitle>Cytokines: regulators of eosinophilic inflammation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>203-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Cytokines are glycoproteins that are secreted and that regulate immunologic inflammation. The cytokine system is characterized by much redundancy and cross-reactivity. Of the more than 100 cytokines that have been identified, interleukin-5 (IL-5) and the chemokine (chemotactic cytokine), eotaxin, are the most selective for cells of eosinophilic origin. Because of this relative specificity, and because of their important immunoregulatory roles, IL-5, eotaxin, and their receptors IL-5R and CCR3 are potential targets for non-glucocorticosteroid pharmacological treatment of eosinophilic inflammation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
